# warnings.topic -- the warnings, Botlish's built-in linter: one topic per
# warning code (hir/warnings.tcl's pass registry; tools/compilerpedia.tcl
# check fails for a registered warning without its topic, its five required
# sections and examples both ways), and the linter's policy. The @nowarn
# examples are the precision corpus: the legitimate cases each rule must stay
# silent on (COMPILERPEDIA.md, "The warning precision corpus").

@topic lint.policy
title: Warning modes: default, off, error
summary: Warnings are on by default and form one global policy per compilation, -warnings default|off|error; there are no per-warning switches and no source suppression.
index: lint warnings backends
alias: warning modes; -warnings; warnings off; warnings as errors; Werror; disable warnings; suppress warning; lint policy
keywords: policy, mode, BOTLISH_WARNINGS, stderr, suppression, pragma
related: lint.warnings-vs-errors principle.lint-precision principle.investigate-warnings cli.options
docs: WARNINGS-SAME-RETURN.md REFACTOR-WARNINGS-CLEAN.md

== THE MODES
    -warnings default   warnings are found and printed on stderr; the
                        program compiles and runs (the default)
    -warnings off       no warning pass runs at all (generated source,
                        benchmarks, embedding)
    -warnings error     the first warning is a compilation error carrying
                        the warning's own code, e.g.
                        (CORE SEMANTIC METHOD-ELIGIBLE), raised before any
                        backend runs

A compilation given no -warnings option uses the environment's
BOTLISH_WARNINGS (default, off or error), else default. Warnings are about
the source program, computed once, before any backend: every backend sees
the same warnings, and warnings never change what the program does. A
program with an error is not warned about.

== WHAT BOTLISH DELIBERATELY DOES NOT HAVE
No per-warning switches (-Wfoo, -Wno-foo, -Werror=foo), no groups (-Wall),
no levels, no lint-ignore comment, pragma or annotation. Every warning is on
for everyone, so a warning must be precise enough that a programmer normally
benefits from investigating it; where the compiler cannot establish its
fact, it is silent (principle.lint-precision). Two declarations look like
opt-outs and are not: `nomethod fn` changes what call forms are legal
(concept.nomethod), and a list result type `-> List[T]` is a real type the
checker proves (warning.fixed-arity-list-return). Both state something true
about a function's interface.

== THE SHIPPED CODE
The standard library, examples and benchmarks compile clean under these
warnings, except the findings a reviewed manifest lists with the reason each
stays open (audit/refactor/manifest.txt in a checkout). A library's warnings
print for every program that loads it; of the shipped modules only
stdlib/web.bot and stdlib/linux/path.bot carry any
(ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL).

== HELP LINES
After the warnings of a compilation, the compiler prints one line per
distinct warning code naming its topic here, e.g.
    help: botlish -help warning.method-eligible

@topic lint.warnings-vs-errors
title: Errors, warnings and principles
summary: An error rejects an invalid program; a warning marks a pattern in a valid program that is usually worth investigating; a principle is advice the compiler may not be able to check at all.
index: lint principles warnings errors
alias: errors versus warnings; error or warning; what is a warning; linter; lint
keywords: difference, rule, advice, invalid, valid
related: lint.policy principle.lint-precision index.errors index.principles

== ERRORS
An error is a violation of a language or compilation requirement: a name
that is not bound, a value that cannot be proven to have its declared type,
a declared error nobody handles, a syntax error. The program is rejected
(exit status 1) and nothing runs. -help errors lists every code.

== WARNINGS
A warning is a pattern that may be undesirable in a program that is valid,
backed by a fact the compiler proves -- "this call is provably the same as
its method spelling", "these two exits return the same value". It is a
claim that the code is worth a second look, with a stated reason; it is
not a claim that the code is wrong. Every warning page says what the rule
detects, what problem it is meant to prevent, the evidence the compiler
uses and the legitimate cases it cannot tell apart. -help warnings lists
them.

== PRINCIPLES
A principle is a recommended practice: name the parts of a multi-value
result, keep a function's receiver first, prefer declared errors to
sentinel values. Some principles are backed by a warning, most are not:
not every good practice can be detected with enough precision to warn
about. -help principles lists them.

== HOW THEY RELATE
A principle explains *why*; a warning is the subset of a principle the
compiler can detect precisely; an error is a rule the language requires.
When a warning fires on code that is reasonable, the warning -- not the
code -- may be too broad: that is evidence to report, not a pattern to
silence mechanically (principle.lint-precision).

@topic warning.same-return-value
title: SAME-RETURN-VALUE: several exits return one proven value
summary: Two or more distinct exits of a function are proven to return the same value: the control flow separates cases that the result does not.
index: warnings lint
code: SAME-RETURN-VALUE
code: CORE SEMANTIC SAME-RETURN-VALUE
alias: same return value; repeated return value; returned from distinct exits
keywords: return, exit, sentinel, guard, early return, redundant branch
related: warning.same-failure principle.guard-clauses principle.errors-over-sentinels principle.lint-precision proof.exact-values
docs: WARNINGS-SAME-RETURN.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A function whose exits -- its `return`s and the value its body ends with
(each leaf of a final if/elif/else) -- include two or more that the compiler
proves return the same value: the same exact constant (`-1`, `false`, `[]`,
through immutable aliases such as `invalid = -1`) or the same immutable
binding. One warning per repeated value, at its first exit, with the other
exits as notes:
    f.bot:3:9: warning: value `-1` is returned from 2 distinct exits
        (SAME-RETURN-VALUE)
    f.bot:5:9: note: also returned here

== WHY IT MATTERS
Each exit of a function is a decision the reader has to follow. When two
paths end with the same value, either the branching that separates them does
not matter to the result -- redundant control flow, a condition the reader
studies for nothing -- or one in-band value stands for several situations
(a sentinel), which the caller can no longer tell apart. Both are worth a
second look: the first usually simplifies to one condition or one exit, the
second may be a failure in disguise that a declared error would name.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Investigate what the repetition means:
  * the branches do not affect the result: merge them (`if a or b:`, drop
    an `if` whose arms agree, let the paths fall through to one exit);
  * the value is a sentinel for "no result": consider a declared error
    (`fail NotFound`), which the caller must handle and which names the
    situation (principle.errors-over-sentinels);
  * the repetition is the honest answer (two situations really have one
    result): keep it; the warning states a fact, not a defect.
A kept finding stays: there is no way to silence one warning (no
annotation, no per-code switch), by design (lint.policy). The program
compiles and runs as usual; only -warnings error rejects it, and
-warnings off turns every warning of the compilation off.

== EVIDENCE
The compiler's exact-value facts (hir::exact): equal exact values through
immutable aliases, or the very same immutable binding. A call -- however
identical it looks --, a parameter-dependent expression or two separately
built mutable values are never assumed equal. An exit counts only if it can
execute: statically decided branches and branches the range facts prove
infeasible are not exits. A nested function's exits are its own.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Recognized, and therefore silent:
  * unit: procedural functions end in unit from many places; nothing is
    collapsed.
  * a Bool answered from several exits beside an exit of the other value:
    Bool has two values, so a predicate with three or more exits -- the
    guard style, `if bad: return false` per condition, then `true` --
    repeats one by necessity, and the repetition says nothing about the
    function. A Bool is reported only when it is the function's only
    answer (every value exit returns it).
  * the handlers of one call: each declared error has its own `on NAME:`
    (there is no `on A, B:`), so `on A: return []` / `on B: return []` is
    one decision written once per error. Exits that end the handlers of
    one call count as one exit.
Not recognized: guard clauses that return the same non-Bool sentinel
(`return -1` from two guards). The compiler cannot tell a deliberate shared
result from a collapsed failure there; the warning asks the question, and
"this really is one result" is a valid answer.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn classify(x: int) -> int:
    if x < 0:
        return -1
    if x > 100:
        return -1
    x

classify(5)
@caption One sentinel for two situations
@warn SAME-RETURN-VALUE
@value 5
@end

@example accept
fn valid?(xs: List[int]) -> bool:
    if xs == []:
        return false
    if xs == [0]:
        return false
    true

valid?([1])
@caption A guard-style predicate: repeated false beside true is not reported
@nowarn SAME-RETURN-VALUE
@value true
@end

@example accept
error Unsolvable
error LimitExceeded

fn solve(n: int) -> List[int] errors Unsolvable, LimitExceeded:
    if n < 0:
        fail Unsolvable
    if n > 40:
        fail LimitExceeded
    [n]

fn solution(n: int) -> List[int]:
    moves = solve(n):
        on Unsolvable:
            return []
        on LimitExceeded:
            return []
    moves

solution(3)
@caption The handlers of one call returning one value are one exit
@nowarn SAME-RETURN-VALUE
@value [3]
@end

@example accept
fn always?(x: int) -> bool:
    if x < 0:
        return false
    false

always?(1)
@caption A predicate that can only answer false is reported
@warn SAME-RETURN-VALUE
@value false
@end

@topic warning.same-failure
title: SAME-FAILURE: several exits fail the same declared error
summary: Two or more distinct `fail` statements of a function raise the same declared error (with a proven-identical payload): the caller cannot tell the situations apart.
index: warnings lint
code: SAME-FAILURE
code: CORE SEMANTIC SAME-FAILURE
alias: same failure; repeated fail
keywords: fail, error, exit, guard, translate errors
related: warning.same-return-value concept.errors concept.error-payloads principle.errors-over-sentinels rule.one-handler-per-error
docs: WARNINGS-SAME-FAILURE.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A function whose own body contains two or more reachable `fail NAME`
statements of the same declared error -- and, for an error with a payload,
with payloads the compiler proves identical. One warning per repeated
failure, at its first `fail`, the others as notes.

== WHY IT MATTERS
SAME-RETURN-VALUE's question for the other way a function ends. A declared
error is part of the function's contract: callers handle it by name. When
two different situations fail with the same error and nothing that tells
them apart, the caller -- and the person reading a report -- cannot know
which one happened. Either the guards could be one guard, or the failures
deserve names (two errors, or one error with a payload field saying which
problem it was: ERROR-PAYLOADS.md).

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Ask whether a caller would want to tell the situations apart. If yes, give
them distinct errors or a payload (`fail Invalid {problem: Problem::Empty}`;
payloads that differ are not grouped). If the situations are one failure
for every caller, merge the guards or keep the uniform failure -- that is a
legitimate answer.

== EVIDENCE
The written `fail` statements of the function's own body (branches, loops,
handler bodies; never a nested function's), the error's resolved identity,
the payload's exact identity, and reachability (decided branches and range
facts). A call that propagates a callee's error is not a `fail` of this
function.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Recognized: translating a call's errors into one of the function's own,
`on A: fail Bad` / `on B: fail Bad`. Each error has its own handler, so the
translation is one decision written once per error; fails that end the
handlers of one call count as one exit. Not recognized: guards that fail
the same error for different reasons -- the warning cannot know whether
callers care, and a uniform failure is a valid answer.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
error Invalid

fn parse(text: str, pos: int) -> int errors Invalid:
    if text == "":
        fail Invalid
    if pos == 3:
        fail Invalid
    pos + 1

"ab".parse(1):
    on Invalid:
        0
@caption Two guards fail the same error with nothing to tell them apart
@warn SAME-FAILURE
@value 2
@end

@example accept
error A
error B
error Bad

fn risky(x: int) -> int errors A, B:
    if x < 0:
        fail A
    if x > 100:
        fail B
    x

fn checked(x: int) -> int errors Bad:
    r = risky(x):
        on A:
            fail Bad
        on B:
            fail Bad
    r

checked(5):
    on Bad:
        0
@caption Translating a call's errors: one decision per handled call
@nowarn SAME-FAILURE
@value 5
@end

@topic warning.method-eligible
title: METHOD-ELIGIBLE: a call that method syntax would express
summary: A call written f(x, y) to a named function of two or more parameters is proven to mean exactly x.f(y): Botlish's preferred receiver form.
index: warnings lint
code: METHOD-ELIGIBLE
code: CORE SEMANTIC METHOD-ELIGIBLE
alias: method eligible; eligible for method syntax; method syntax warning
keywords: method, receiver, sugar, functional call, nomethod
related: concept.method-sugar concept.nomethod rule.method-resolution principle.receiver-first principle.lint-precision
docs: WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md METHOD-SUGAR.md REFACTOR-WARNINGS-CLEAN.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A call written in functional form, `f(x, y)` or `std::list::at(xs, i)`,
whose method spelling `x.f(y)` / `xs.at(i)` the compiler proves to parse and
to resolve to the identical function, where the function is a declared named
function (not a function value) with at least two ordinary parameters, not
declared `nomethod`. One warning per call:
    f.bot:2:12: warning: call to `std::list::at` is eligible for method syntax
        (METHOD-ELIGIBLE)

== WHY IT MATTERS
Receiver-oriented calls make the relationship between an operation and its
primary value visible: `cells.at(i)`, `name.concat(suffix)`, `board.move(d)`
read as "this value, this operation", chain left to right
(`text.trim().concat(x)` instead of nested calls read inside out), and put
the subject first where the eye starts. Botlish chose method syntax as its
preferred call form (METHOD-SUGAR.md) and made it exact sugar -- the same
function, the same HIR -- so writing it costs nothing and the warning can
promise that the rewrite changes nothing but the spelling.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Prefer the method spelling when the first argument is the value the
operation is about. When the first parameter is *not* a receiver -- the
function is procedural or symmetric, or its first argument is a parameter of
the operation rather than its subject (a name, a position, a count, a
destination) -- declare the function `nomethod fn`: that is a statement
about its interface (method syntax becomes an error for it,
NOMETHOD-CALL), not a way to silence a call site. The shipped library marks
bit_and, bit_or, bit_xor (symmetric), std::mutable_array::copy (destination
first) and std::mutable_array::create (length first).

== EVIDENCE
The compiler's own parser rule for what may precede a `.` (a name, a field
chain, a call or method-call result, a literal, a list or struct literal --
never an operator expression, which would need parentheses), the resolver's
own method-candidate search from the call's scope (exactly one candidate,
the same function; no struct field of that name that could compete), how
the call was written (the frontend's provenance), and reachability.
Anything it cannot prove is silence.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Recognized, and therefore silent:
  * one-parameter functions: `length(xs)` has nothing to move behind the
    dot.
  * an Int literal receiver: `register_tile(0, 0, "x")`,
    `scan_while(0, pred)`. A written number is almost always a constant
    parameter of an operation on something else -- a position, a count, a
    size -- and `0.scan_while(pred)` presents it as the subject; whether a
    constant is the subject is a judgment the compiler cannot make
    (`5.between(1, 10)` stays legal, just not suggested).
  * function values (parameters, aliases) and `nomethod` functions.
Not recognized: whether a parameter is a natural receiver in general. A
helper `label(prefix, n)` or `register(name, rows)` called with a String
first warns; the structure of the call cannot tell a label from a text
being operated on (`"move: ".concat(name)` is a natural receiver). That is
the author's decision, made once at the declaration with `nomethod`.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn first(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    std::list::at(xs, 0):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1

first([7, 8])
@caption The receiver is the list the operation reads
@warn METHOD-ELIGIBLE
@value 7
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn first(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    xs.at(0):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1

first([7, 8])
@caption The method spelling: the same call
@nowarn METHOD-ELIGIBLE
@value 7
@end

@example accept
fn tile_name(row: int, column: int) -> int:
    row * 10 + column

tile_name(0, 3)
@caption An Int literal receiver is not suggested
@nowarn METHOD-ELIGIBLE
@value 3
@end

@example accept
import std::str

nomethod fn label(prefix: str, n: str) -> str:
    prefix.concat(n)

label("legal moves at ", "12")
@caption A function whose first parameter is not a receiver, declared so
@nowarn METHOD-ELIGIBLE
@value "legal moves at 12"
@end

@topic warning.fixed-arity-list-return
title: FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN: a function returns positional multi-value lists
summary: Every value exit of a function returns a written list literal of one arity N >= 2: a multi-value result whose parts have positions instead of names.
index: warnings lint
code: FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN
code: CORE SEMANTIC FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN
alias: fixed arity list return; tuple return; positional result
keywords: list, tuple, struct, multi-value, result, named parts
related: principle.name-the-parts concept.structs concept.destructuring rule.no-list-destructuring concept.lists
docs: WARNINGS-FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN.md MULTI-VALUE-RESULTS.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A function none of whose value exits is anything but a written list literal
of the same arity N >= 2 (or a binding of one, or a direct self-call), with
no list type declared for its result. One warning per function, at its
first exit:
    t.bot:10:13: warning: a 2-element list is returned from all 3 value
        exits; a struct value names the parts (FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN)

== WHY IT MATTERS
A result like `[field, index + 1]` is a pair whose parts the caller reads by
position: `std::list::at(r, 0)` and `std::list::at(r, 1)`, each of which
must handle IndexNotFound, none of which says what the part is, and all of
which silently break when the order changes. Botlish deliberately gives
Lists no positional destructuring (a List is a sequence) and gives structs
named fields and destructuring: `{field, next} = scan(...)`. A struct result
is checked by name at every use.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Return a struct (an anonymous one is enough: `{field: f, next: i + 1}`) and
destructure it at the call. When the result really is a list -- a row of
data for output, a sequence that happens to have a fixed length -- declare
it: `-> list` or `-> List[T]`. That annotation is a real type the checker
proves, and it tells the reader the positions are a sequence, not parts.

== EVIDENCE
How each exit was written (the frontend records a written list literal),
binding identity for exits that read a binding of a literal, the resolver's
identity for self-calls, reachability, and the function's declared result
type. Element types are never consulted.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
A genuine fixed-length sequence (rows of a table, a coordinate written as a
list for an external format): the author says so with the list result
type, which is the language's statement of intent, not a suppression.
Silent by construction: any exit that is computed, a struct, unit (a
"list or nothing" result), a call of another function, or of another
arity; one-element lists. The warning cannot know how callers use the
result; that is why the declaration decides.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn split_at(text: str, i: int):
    if i < 0:
        return [text, 0]
    [text, i]

"ab".split_at(1)
@caption Two parts, read by position at every use
@warn FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN
@value ["ab", 1]
@end

@example accept
fn split_at(text: str, i: int):
    if i < 0:
        return {text: text, at: 0}
    {text: text, at: i}

{text, at} = "ab".split_at(1)
at
@caption A struct names the parts
@nowarn FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN
@value 1
@end

@example accept
nomethod fn row(name: str, solved: bool) -> list:
    [name, solved, 16]

row("solved board", true)
@caption A declared list result: a row of data for output
@nowarn FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN
@value ["solved board", true, 16]
@end

@topic warning.proves-naming
title: PROVES-NAMING: a proof-producing function named outside its convention
summary: A function with a `proves` contract of one of the two refinement shapes is not named by that shape's convention: a predicate ends in ?, a validator is validate or validate_*.
index: warnings lint
code: PROVES-NAMING
code: CORE SEMANTIC PROVES-NAMING
alias: proves naming; predicate name; validator name
keywords: proves, refinement, predicate, validator, naming, question mark
related: concept.refinements principle.predicate-naming
docs: WARNINGS-PROVES-NAMING.md REFINEMENT-VALUES.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A function whose declaration carries a `proves PARAM: Type` clause and has
exactly one ordinary parameter, and is either a predicate (result `bool`)
whose name does not end in `?`, or a validator (result `unit`) whose name is
neither `validate` nor begins with `validate_`. One warning at the `fn`:
    f.bot:2:1: warning: `emailish` proves a contract and returns bool;
        predicate names end in `?` (PROVES-NAMING)

== WHY IT MATTERS
A refinement's proof is invisible at the call: `if emailish?(s):` narrows
`s` to `Emailish` in the branch, and `validate_emailish(s)` makes `s` an
`Emailish` for the rest of the path. The name is the only thing at the call
that tells the reader a proof is being made and which kind: a `?` reads as a
question whose true answer proves something, `validate_` as a step that
either establishes the fact or fails. A proof-producing function named like
an ordinary computation hides the fact the code below depends on.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Rename: `emailish?`, `validate_emailish` (or `validate` in a module whose
namespace names the subject). A rename is the whole response; it changes
nothing else.

== EVIDENCE
The resolved proof contract of each declaration, its ordinary parameter
count (flags and context parameters do not count), its declared result and
the name as written. No call site, no walk.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
None within its scope: the scope guard is the boundary. A function without
`proves` is never looked at, whatever its name (`is_empty` is fine), and a
proves function with two or more ordinary parameters is outside the two
shapes and silent. There is no reverse rule: a `?` name that returns an
Int is not reported.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
refined type Positive = int

fn positive(n: int) -> bool proves n: Positive:
    n > 0

positive(3)
@caption A predicate named like a computation
@warn PROVES-NAMING
@value true
@end

@example accept
refined type Positive = int

fn positive?(n: int) -> bool proves n: Positive:
    n > 0

positive?(3)
@caption The predicate convention
@nowarn PROVES-NAMING
@value true
@end

@topic warning.one-char-string-literal
title: ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL: a String literal of one character
summary: A written String literal whose value is exactly one character: a single character is written as a character literal ('x'), a value of its own type.
index: warnings lint
code: ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
code: CORE SEMANTIC ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
alias: one char string literal; one character string; single character string
keywords: character, UnicodeChar, char literal, String, append_char, char_at
related: principle.characters-not-strings concept.strings stdlib.std::str::append_char stdlib.std::str::char_at
docs: WARNINGS-ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL.md ENCODE-UTF8-ALLOCATION-RESEARCH.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
Every written String literal whose exact value is one character, measured as
std::str::length measures (escapes resolved: "\n" and "\"" are one
character; a supplementary-plane character is one; a letter plus a combining
accent is two). One warning per literal, wherever it is written:
    f.bot:5:21: warning: the String literal `"\""` is one character long; a
        single character is written as a character literal
        (ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL)

== WHY IT MATTERS
Botlish has a character type, UnicodeChar ('a', '\n'), distinct from String.
A one-character String is a String: classifying text by comparing
one-character Strings allocates a String per position on the native
backends and states a character question in String terms. The character
idiom -- `text.char_at(i) == '%'`, `text.append_char('\n')` -- says what is
meant and allocates nothing.

== TEXT OR CODE POINTS
The warning is not a cosmetic preference for 'x' over "x". Both are
values, of different kinds: "x" is text, 'x' is one Unicode code point (a
UnicodeChar, concept.strings). It asks which is meant where the two are
easy to blur. A separator such as a space or a newline given to an API that
works on a code point (append_char, a comparison with char_at) is usually
meant as the UnicodeChar. The warning itself reports one-character String
literals only; a String of any other length is never reported.

Semantic reason. A String literal is compact, which makes it a tempting
spelling for a collection of symbols, but it says "text". "ULDR" is text.
['U', 'L', 'D', 'R'] is an addressable sequence of code points: its
elements are enumerated by `loop` and read as list elements, whereas a
String is read through char_at and cannot be iterated by `loop`. Use the
String when the value is notation that is read, printed or concatenated;
use the List when the program treats its elements as symbols. Neither is
better in general, and a symbolic sequence is not always a List: the choice
follows whether the value is conceptually text.

@example accept
import std::str

notation = "ULDR"
moves = ['U', 'L', 'D', 'R']
ups = loop m in moves:
    m == 'U'
[notation.length(), ups]
@caption The same four symbols as text, and as a sequence the program enumerates
@value [4, [true, false, false, false]]
@nowarn ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
@quiet
@end

Performance reason. The native backends store a String's text as UTF-8
(the language promises only a sequence of scalar values). Reading text as
code points decodes it -- for non-ASCII text, finding character i means
decoding forward -- and building text from code points encodes it. A
List[UnicodeChar] already holds the scalar values. Converting text to code
points, manipulating them and encoding the result back is work that data
which was a code-point sequence from the start does not need. No figure is
claimed here: the cost depends on the program and the backend.

C programmers draw a similar line when they choose between a string
literal and a brace initializer for a char array.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Write characters as character literals and use the character operations:
std::str::char_at to read one, std::str::append_char to append one, ==
between characters to compare. Where an API you call takes a String for
what is really a character, the finding is about that API; changing the
API (to take a UnicodeChar) is the response the warning is meant to prompt.

== EVIDENCE
The literal's own exact value and its length in Unicode scalar values. The
warning is deliberately context-blind: it never looks at what consumes the
literal, follows no value, evaluates no call, and has no reachability rule.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Not recognized, by design: a one-character String that really is text (a
separator passed to a function that takes a String, a table of
one-character Strings). The warning fires there too, because those
findings are its purpose: they catalogue the APIs that take a String where
a character is meant. It is also deliberately not autofixable: replacing
"x" by 'x' changes the program where the consumer takes a String (a type
error, a run-time error, or a comparison of a String with a character that
is silently false). The shipped library's open findings are of this kind
(stdlib/web.bot, stdlib/linux/path.bot).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::str

fn quoted?(text: str, i: int) -> bool:
    s = text.substring(i, i + 1):
        on LowerUnderrun:
            return false
        on UpperOverrun:
            return false
    s == "\""

"a\"b".quoted?(1)
@caption A character question asked in String terms
@warn ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
@value true
@end

@example accept
import std::str

fn quoted?(text: str, i: int) -> bool:
    c = text.char_at(i):
        on IndexNotFound:
            return false
    c == '"'

"a\"b".quoted?(1)
@caption The character idiom
@nowarn ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
@value true
@end

@topic warning.many-boolean-arguments
title: MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS: a call passes several boolean literals
summary: A written call passes two or more true/false literals to a function that takes a subject and boolean options: anonymous options that flags would name.
index: warnings lint
code: MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS
code: CORE SEMANTIC MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS
alias: many boolean arguments; boolean literals arguments; boolean trap
keywords: bool, true, false, flags, options, arguments
related: concept.flags principle.options-are-flags rule.flags
docs: WARNINGS-MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS.md FLAGS.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A written call that passes two or more `true`/`false` literals at ordinary
argument positions to a declared function (or a native) whose parameters
include at least two proven `bool` -- declared, or a trusted inferred
type -- and at least one that is not (a subject). One warning per call:
    f.bot:4:1: warning: the call to `open_file` passes 3 boolean literals;
        flags name options (MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS)

== WHY IT MATTERS
`open_file("f.txt", true, false, true)` cannot be read without the
declaration: `true` carries no name, and swapping two of them is a silent,
type-correct bug. Botlish has flags for options:
`fn open_file(path, flags :append, :create, :sync)`, called
`open_file("f.txt", :append, :sync)` -- every option named where it is
used, absent options false.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
Give the function flag parameters for its options. Values the caller
computes or names (`append: should_append`) already say what they mean and
are never counted.

== EVIDENCE
The call's written arguments (a `true`/`false` token only; a named binding
or a computed bool never counts), the callee's declared or trusted
parameter types, flags excluded, and structural reachability.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Silent by construction: a function whose parameters are all bool
(`xor(a: bool, b: bool)` takes data, not options), a single literal, calls
through function values, untyped (only run-time-checked) bool parameters.
Not recognized: a function whose bools are data despite the subject
(rare); flags are then still the clearer interface, or name the values with
bindings.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn open_file(path: str, append: bool, create: bool) -> str:
    path

"log.txt".open_file(true, false)
@caption Two anonymous options
@warn MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS
@value "log.txt"
@end

@example accept
fn open_file(path: str, flags :append, :create) -> str:
    path

open_file("log.txt", :append)
@caption Flags name the options
@nowarn MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS
@value "log.txt"
@end

@topic warning.context-resource-escape
title: CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE: an airlock hands out its raw foreign resource
summary: An airlock -- a module mediating raw authority through its own context -- passes a C pointer, a native view or edit, or an open struct holding one, to another module.
index: warnings lint
code: CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE
alias: context resource escape; airlock leak; raw pointer escape; resource escape; leaked handle
keywords: airlock, context, pointer, Ptr, FnPtr, NativeView, NativeEdit, opaque, private, raw, foreign, resource, contextualized, SDL
related: concept.private concept.contexts concept.direct-modules concept.opaque-structs concept.c-ffi warning.many-boolean-arguments
docs: MODULE-PRIVACY-AND-CONTEXT-ESCAPE-MILESTONE-1.md ICY-TOWER-CONTEXT-REFACTOR-CENSUS.md DIRECT-MODULES.md

== WHAT IT DETECTS
A module is an *airlock* when the compiler proves it contextualized
(concept.direct-modules): it makes raw calls -- a retaining C call, a
native view or edit -- and a context the module declares mediates every
one. The warning fires when code of another module takes the airlock's raw
representation across that boundary:
  * R2: calling a public function of the airlock (or taking it as a value)
    passes in or receives a C address (`Ptr`, `FnPtr`), alone or inside a
    List, vector, array or struct whose fields the caller may read;
  * R3: the same with a `NativeView` or `NativeEdit`;
  * R4: the caller constructs, destructures or reads the fields of a
    non-opaque struct of the airlock that holds one.
One warning per exposing declaration -- the function or the struct, where
the fix is -- naming each consuming module, its number of crossings and its
first one:
    gfx.bot:7:1: warning: gfx::renderer exposes a raw C pointer (its
        result: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]) across the boundary of
        module "gfx", which mediates its raw authority through its context
        gfx::Gfx: the entry program receives one (1 call; first at
        main.bot:5:9). ... (CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE)

== WHY IT MATTERS
An airlock exists to keep raw authority behind a capability. A raw pointer
or a native view it hands out carries that authority past the context: an
importer can pass the pointer to C, read every pointer a view's memory
holds, or keep it after the airlock closed it -- none of which needs
authority of its own, and none of which the module statuses show
(directness tracks who makes raw calls, not who holds raw handles). Icy
Tower's first SDL wrapper did exactly this: 38 calls carried renderer
pointers through the game, with 56 clones and every frame loop exit
threading the window back to close it.

== RECOMMENDED PRACTICE
The airlock principle: raw foreign authority belongs in inspectable
architectural boundaries, and a context ordinarily exposes what callers
are allowed to do, not the raw representation with which it does it.
  * Keep the resource behind the context and give the module operations
    that take and return values (`gfx::fill(x, y, w, h)`).
  * Inside the airlock, make the helpers that take or return pointers and
    views `private` (concept.private): they stay implementation.
  * Or seal the resource in an opaque struct: a public opaque handle is an
    intentional abstraction, and carrying it is no escape.

== EVIDENCE
The direct-module analysis's contextualized status of the declaring module;
the checked types at each crossing (arguments, result, value) and of each
construction, projection or destructuring; opacity and privacy as declared.
A crossing is a reference from another unit to a public member of the
airlock: a private member never is one. A struct opaque or private to
another module stops the search for raw representation.

== LEGITIMATE EXCEPTIONS
Silent by construction: calls inside the airlock; private helpers; public
opaque resource values; modules that are not contextualized -- a designated
`direct module` is a raw layer by declaration, and an ordinary module
mediates nothing; values without raw representation. A pointer passed *in*
by a module that has raw authority of its own (direct, or an airlock
itself) is taken to be that module's own resource and is not reported. Not
recognized: an airlock that deliberately lends a caller-owned buffer to an
ordinary module and takes it back; a trait operation's crossing (its types
are the trait's contract).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import gfx

with context gfx::create()

fn frame():
    r = gfx::renderer()
    r.fill(3)

0
@module gfx
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn gfx_open() -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]
extern c fn gfx_fill(renderer: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void], x: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int

opaque context struct Gfx:
    ready: bool

fn create() -> Gfx:
    Gfx {ready: true}

fn renderer(context g: Gfx) -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]:
    gfx_open()

fn fill(renderer: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void], x: std::ffi::c::Int, context g: Gfx) -> std::ffi::c::Int:
    renderer.gfx_fill(x)
@caption The airlock's renderer pointer crosses into the application
@warn CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE
@value 0
@end

@example accept
import gfx

with context gfx::create()

fn frame():
    gfx::fill(3)

0
@module gfx
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn gfx_open() -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]
extern c fn gfx_fill(renderer: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void], x: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int

opaque context struct Gfx:
    ready: bool

fn create() -> Gfx:
    Gfx {ready: true}

private fn renderer(context g: Gfx) -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]:
    gfx_open()

fn fill(x: std::ffi::c::Int, context g: Gfx) -> std::ffi::c::Int:
    r = renderer()
    r.gfx_fill(x)
@caption A private helper returns the pointer; the public operation takes values
@nowarn CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE
@value 0
@end

@example accept
import gfx

with context gfx::create()

fn frame():
    s = gfx::open()
    s.fill(3)

0
@module gfx
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn gfx_open() -> std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]
extern c fn gfx_fill(renderer: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void], x: std::ffi::c::Int) -> std::ffi::c::Int

opaque context struct Gfx:
    ready: bool

opaque struct Surface:
    renderer: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void]

fn create() -> Gfx:
    Gfx {ready: true}

fn open(context g: Gfx) -> Surface:
    Surface {renderer: gfx_open()}

fn fill(s: Surface, x: std::ffi::c::Int, context g: Gfx) -> std::ffi::c::Int:
    {renderer} = s
    renderer.gfx_fill(x)
@caption A public opaque resource value carries the pointer sealed
@nowarn CONTEXT-RESOURCE-ESCAPE
@value 0
@end

